---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title={'Teaching — Marco Sanchioni'} description={'Courses taught by Marco Sanchioni at the Sophia University Institute, at the intersection of physics and philosophy.'}>
<section class="page-hero">
    <div class="glow glow--accent glow--a" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="wrap reveal">
      <span class="eyebrow">Teaching</span>
      <h1>Courses</h1>
      <p class="lead serif" style="max-width:58ch;">At the Sophia University Institute, teaching across the intersection of physics, cosmology and the philosophy of nature.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band band--paper2">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-intro reveal"><h2>Current &amp; Recent Courses</h2></div>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2025</span>
        <h3>Filosofia del Linguaggio</h3>
        <p class="inst">MA course · Sophia University Institute</p>
      </article>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2025</span>
        <h3>Laboratorio Interdisciplinare di Fede e Scienza</h3>
        <p class="inst">MA course · Sophia University Institute</p>
      </article>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2024</span>
        <h3>Cosmologia e Filosofia della Natura</h3>
        <p class="inst">MA course · Sophia University Institute</p>
        <p>Il corso esamina le connessioni tra fisica e filosofia all'interno della cosmologia, studiando l'origine e l'evoluzione dell'universo. Temi centrali: il Big Bang, le ipotesi sul "prima", l'emergenza della complessità da stati semplici e le implicazioni filosofiche del modello ΛCDM. L'approccio è interdisciplinare, combinando analisi scientifica e riflessione sulla struttura del reale cosmico.</p>
        <div class="links" style="margin-top:1rem; font-family:var(--display); font-weight:500;">
          <a href="https://www.canva.com/design/DAGEFY2gRQs/NQOIpyjTxZc7zGoxx6JAVw/view" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Slides ↗</a>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2023</span>
        <h3>Genesis and Figures of Modern Scientific Rationality</h3>
        <p class="inst">MA course · Sophia University Institute</p>
        <p>A historical-theoretical analysis of the emergence of modern scientific rationality from its roots in philosophical rationality. The course focuses on the philosophy of nature in the 17th-century European context, examining the distinctive features of scientific rationality — autonomy, methodology, and its relations with metaphysics and other domains of knowledge. Central figures include Galileo, Descartes, Newton and Leibniz.</p>
      </article>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2021</span>
        <h3>Philosophy of Dualities</h3>
        <p class="inst">12-hour seminar series · University of Bologna</p>
      </article>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2020</span>
        <h3>Philosophy of Black Holes</h3>
        <p class="inst">12-hour seminar series · University of Bologna</p>
      </article>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2016</span>
        <h3>Supersymmetric Field Theory</h3>
        <p class="inst">30-hour course · MSc Theoretical Physics, Niels Bohr Institute, Copenhagen</p>
      </article>

      <article class="course reveal">
        <span class="term">2015–2016</span>
        <h3>General Relativity</h3>
        <p class="inst">30-hour course · MSc Theoretical Physics, Niels Bohr Institute, Copenhagen</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SUPERVISION -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-intro reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">Supervision</span>
        <h2>Doctoral &amp; Research Mentoring</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="about-grid">
        <div class="reveal">
          <p style="max-width:65ch; color:var(--ink-soft); font-size:1rem; line-height:1.7;">
            As Director of the Doctoral School at the Sophia University Institute, I supervise
            doctoral students and early-career researchers working at the intersection of physics
            and philosophy. My mentoring combines sustained attention to technical rigour in the
            formalism with a training in explicit conceptual argumentation — the same orientation
            that structures the research programme.
          </p>
          <div class="info-block" style="margin-top:1.6rem;">
            <h3>Doctoral Candidates</h3>
            <ul class="info-list">
              <li>Lucia Salari — PhD Candidate, Sophia University Institute (since 2024)</li>
              <li>Andrea Galluzzi — PhD Candidate, Sophia University Institute (since 2024)</li>
              <li>Marianne Najm Abou-Jaoude — PhD Candidate, Sophia University Institute (since 2024)</li>
              <li>Post-doctoral evaluation committee: A. Borghesi, Sophia University Institute — 2025–2026</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <p style="max-width:65ch; color:var(--ink-soft); font-size:1rem; line-height:1.7; margin-top:1rem;">
            I welcome inquiries from prospective doctoral candidates working in the philosophy of
            quantum mechanics, quantum gravity, or the metaphysics of physics more broadly. Candidates
            should have some formal training in physics, mathematics or analytic philosophy.
          </p>
          <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:1.8rem;">
            <a href="mailto:marco.sanchioni@sophiauniversity.org" class="btn btn--primary">Get in touch ↗</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <aside class="reveal">
          <div class="info-block">
            <h3>Current Research Topics</h3>
            <ul class="info-list">
              <li>Foundations of relational quantum mechanics</li>
              <li>Ontology of gauge symmetries and quantum field theory</li>
              <li>Black holes and the philosophy of quantum gravity</li>
              <li>Background independence in non-relativistic gravity</li>
              <li>Emergence, effective field theories and the metaphysics of physics</li>
              <li>Scientific realism and underdetermination</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PHILOSOPHY & PHYSICS READING GROUP -->
  <section class="band band--ink">
    <div class="glow glow--silver glow--c" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="wrap reveal" style="max-width:68ch;">
      <span class="eyebrow">Community</span>
      <h2>Philosophy &amp; Physics at Sophia</h2>
      <p class="lead" style="color:rgba(247,245,239,.82); margin-top:1rem;">
        The Sophia University Institute hosts a regular reading group on the philosophy and foundations
        of physics, open to researchers, doctoral students and advanced undergraduates. Sessions are
        held in English and Italian. If you would like to participate or propose a session, please
        get in touch.
      </p>
      <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <a href="mailto:marco.sanchioni@sophiauniversity.org" class="btn btn--primary" style="background:var(--accent); color:var(--ink);">Contact me</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
